use javax.swing.JFrame
use java.awt.GraphicsEnvironment

# C13 the half that needs a real display: realising a window, and having the window system
# actually map it. `c13_window` pins -Djava.awt.headless=true and asserts what a program sees
# when there is no display. This probe does the opposite and asserts the *relationship*
# between the two answers, which is what makes one recorded expectation correct on a macOS
# developer machine and on a Linux CI runner alike.
#
# Printing `headless` or `worked` alone would make the expectation host-dependent and the
# probe would only ever match one machine. Printing whether they agree cannot: a host with a
# display answers false/true and a host without one answers true/false, and both reduce to
# true here. The second line is the guard against the degenerate case where a broken bridge
# returns false for both, which would satisfy `headless == not worked` while proving nothing.
#
# This is not a member of `HEADLESS` in run.py. It must run under whatever the host provides,
# because the invariant is the thing under test.
headless = GraphicsEnvironment.isHeadless()

worked = false
try
  frame = JFrame("c13-display")
  frame.setSize(80, 60)
  frame.setVisible(true)
  worked = true
  frame.dispose()
catch err
  worked = false
end

print headless == not worked
print worked or headless
